# Layers: order by "is above" (transitive closure); a relation that goes up is an error.
The layer "interface" is above the layer "execution".
The layer "execution" is above the layer "domain".
The layer "domain" is above the layer "data".
The program "app" contains the module "cli", the module "executor", the module "rules" and the module "cache".
The module "cli" is in the layer "interface".
The module "executor" is in the layer "execution".
The module "rules" is in the layer "domain".
The module "cache" is in the layer "data".
The module "rules" contains the function "validate".
The module "cli" uses the module "executor" and the module "cache".
The function "validate" reads the module "cache".
# goes up from data to interface and from domain to execution: only the declared exception passes
The module "cache" exceptionally calls the module "cli".
The function "validate" uses the module "executor".
# an exception without a violation is a warning
The module "cli" exceptionally reads the module "rules".
# cycle and two layers
The layer "x" is above the layer "y".
The layer "y" is above the layer "x".
The module "double" is in the layer "x".
The module "double" is in the layer "y".
# 'exceptionally' only in a simple sentence
If the module "cli" stops, the module "cache" exceptionally calls the module "cli".
